From 7ff5369fbd06510a0dfde8b1dbdbb17099d103f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:15:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 47/48] grate: run fragment programs of more than one epoch
 through the sequencer DMA

NVIDIA's driver runs a fragment program that needs more than the 32
constant slots as several epochs: the sequencer loads the program block
and the constant packet of each epoch by DMA, runs the epoch, and keeps
the registers of the pixels in flight in a pixel spill buffer between
the epochs. Add the driver half of it.

grate_fp_info gets an optional struct grate_fp_epochs: per epoch the
program block (offset and length in a program buffer), the instruction
count and a constant packet (bank slot, and for every constant the
uniform word or the immediate it takes). For such a program the compiled
blob holds the common state only (rows, PSEQ_CTL with MIN_OUT 0, the
offset resets); emit_epochs() writes the three command pairs of every
epoch (constant DMA, program DMA, run: the odd slots of the DMAs are
relocations, the other commands write their even slot only), the
closing command pair, the pixel spill addresses and PSEQ_PC. The program
buffer is written on first use and the constant buffer for every draw,
after the wait for the draw that used it last. grate_fp_max_qid() is the
table that program.c had inline.

The Tegra 30 has two pixel pipelines that take alternate swaths of a
draw. Each needs a pixel spill region of its own, and NVIDIA's driver
writes GLOBAL_SPILLSURFADDR once for every pipe with GSHIM_WRITE_MASK
selecting it (NvArdStreamBeginAndPushRelocs, multi-instance branch). One
address broadcast to both pipes lets them overwrite each other's spilled
pixels: whole batches of the pixels in flight were never written (a
contiguous run of quads, 100 to 480 of 1024 pixels, as much as
MAX_OUT / rows), and a draw that follows a finished one hung the job
about half of the time.

Nothing creates such a program yet: the compiler still refuses more than
32 uniform scalars. Blending is not applied to a program of more than one
epoch.

Needs kernel patch 0031 (drivers/gpu/drm/grate) and Mesa 0046. Checked
on the tablet (kernel 7.0.1-r19): NVIDIA's compiled two-epoch d12t
program (49 uniform scalars), injected through a test hook that is not
part of this commit, with the pixels counted that the draw never touched:
one spill address for both pipes loses 100 to 480 of 1024 pixels at
MAX_OUT 0x12c and 0x164 and hangs the job in 8 of 8 runs of four draws
in a row, one spill region per pipe loses none in 10 of 10 runs at each
capacity and in 8 of 8 runs of four draws, without a hang. A pixel spill
address is needed (without it the data cache writes to a stale address and
the job hangs), surface 4 is not. The 21 other self-checking tests give
the same output as before.
